# 7. Starvation ⭐⭐⭐⭐

A thread waits indefinitely because other threads continuously get access to the shared resource.

Example:

A low-priority thread never gets CPU time because high-priority threads always run.

### Solutions

* Fair locks (`ReentrantLock(true)`)
* Fair scheduling

---

# 8. Livelock ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Threads are active but make no progress.

Example:

Two people meet in a narrow corridor.

Person A moves left.

Person B also moves left.

Both then move right.

They keep moving but never pass each other.

Similarly, threads keep responding to each other but never complete their work.

---

# 9. Thread Interference ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Multiple threads modify the same data without proper synchronization, leading to inconsistent results.

This is one of the causes of race conditions.

Example:

```java
count++;
```

executed simultaneously by multiple threads.

---

# 10. Resource Contention ⭐⭐⭐

Many threads compete for the same resource.

Examples:

* Database connection
* Printer
* File
* Shared object

Too much contention reduces performance because threads spend more time waiting.

---

# 11. Thread Leakage ⭐⭐⭐

Threads are created but never terminated.

Example:

```java
while(true) {

    new Thread(...).start();

}
```

Eventually, the JVM may run out of memory or operating system thread limits.

### Solution

Use an `ExecutorService` and properly shut it down.

---

# 12. Context Switching Overhead ⭐⭐⭐

If too many threads are created, the CPU spends significant time switching between them instead of performing useful work.

More threads do not always mean better performance.

### Solution

Use thread pools (`ExecutorService`) instead of creating unlimited threads.

